{
  "_comment": "An invented batch folder reads as here (owner, 2026-10-21). A path with a glob in it (an input or an output) whose folder does not exist where the plan runs, in a request that does not name that folder, reads as the current folder: `videos/*.mov` for \"the .mov videos here\" becomes `*.mov`, before known-folder resolution would point `videos/` at the user's Videos folder. The request names a folder when it writes it with a slash, after in/into/to/from/on/onto/inside (optionally my/the), next to the word folder (`Videos folder`, `folder videos`), or after `folder called` / `folder named`, quoted or not (2026-10-24:\"put them in a folder called out\" lost `out/`); a nested folder only when the request names its whole path, and a resolution or size (`720p`, `1080p`, `4k`, `1920x1080`) is never a folder name, except written with a slash. Paths without a glob, absolute paths, `~` paths, URLs and `$var` references are never changed. `dirs` exist under the folder the plan runs in. Python `knaif.batch_chain.drop_invented_folders`, Rust `knaif_core::batch_chain::drop_invented_folders`.",
  "cases": [
